---
import DocLink from '@/components/DocLink.astro';
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import Choices from '@/components/motion/Choices.astro';
import Choice from '@/components/motion/Choice.astro';
import GoodToKnow from '@/components/motion/GoodToKnow.astro';
import Fact from '@/components/motion/Fact.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Scopes from '@/components/motion/scenes/Scopes.astro';
import { ScopesControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/ScopesControls';
import { glossaryHref } from '@/lib/motion-glossary';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="per-breakpoint"
  title="Motion: per breakpoint"
  description="Motion per breakpoint with --udx-motion and the Tailwind utilities motion-[name] and motion-none, so a phone and a desktop can play different motions. In HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
>
  <Fragment slot="summary">
    Give an element a Tailwind class per breakpoint, such as <code>md:motion-[fade-on-view]</code>, to play another motion
    from that breakpoint up, or <code>motion-none</code> to play none.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="when-to-use-it">When to use it</h2>
  <Choices size="compact">
    <Choice lead title="motion-[name]" href="#set-a-motion-per-breakpoint" link="Set a motion per breakpoint">
      <Fragment slot="when">a motion should change with the screen size: a card that rises on a phone and fades on a desktop, or that plays none on a phone.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">the element keeps its <DocLink href={glossaryHref('trigger')}>triggers</DocLink> and its kind, and plays the named motion in its place from the breakpoint you name.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
    <Choice when="Prefer it when" code={false} title="Reveal" href="/motion/reveal" link="Reveal">
      <Fragment slot="when">the motion is the same at every width, and plays as the element scrolls into view.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">a plain reveal needs no breakpoint: its name in data-motion is the whole setting.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
    <Choice when="Prefer it when" code={false} title="Reduced motion" href="/motion/accessibility" link="Accessibility">
      <Fragment slot="when">the reader has asked for less motion: the policy covers every motion of the page, whatever the screen size.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">one setting of the document reduces them all, with no class to write for each breakpoint.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
  </Choices>

  <h2 id="resize-the-window">Resize the window</h2>
  <p>
    The first card rises at every width. The second rises below <code>md</code> (768 px), and fades from <code>md</code> up. The third has
    <code>motion-none</code>, so it does not move. Replay plays the three again.
  </p>
  <MotionStage scene="scopes" label="Live example: three cards, each with its motion per breakpoint" height={[240, 408]}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><ScopesControls client:visible /></Fragment>
    <Scopes size="page" />
  </MotionStage>

  <h2 id="set-a-motion-per-breakpoint">Set a motion per breakpoint</h2>
  <p>
    A Tailwind class sets <code>--udx-motion</code> on an element: <code>motion-[name]</code> names a defined motion, and
    <code>motion-none</code> names none. Put a variant in front to limit it to a breakpoint: <code>md:motion-[fade-on-view]</code> applies
    from <code>md</code> up.
  </p>
  <p>
    At each trigger (an entry, an exit, a replay, or a loop that starts or resumes), the element plays the named motion in place of its own.
    The named motion must be of the same kind: a reveal replaces a reveal, and a presence replaces a presence. A name of another kind, or one
    that is not defined, is ignored: the element plays its own motion, and a development build reports it once.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <DocLink href={glossaryHref('binding')}>binding</DocLink> keeps its own triggers, and for a reveal, its own <code>axis</code> and
    <code>repeat</code>. To read which motion plays on a page, see <DocLink href="/motion/inspect">Inspect and revert</DocLink>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>fade-on-view</code> is a reveal that fades, defined with <code>defineMotion</code>: a presence such as <code>fade</code> would be
    ignored on a card that rises. The example sets the three cards of the scene, in each framework.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-scopes-breakpoints" live={false} />

  <h2 id="options">Options</h2>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: '--udx-motion', values: 'none · a defined motion of the same kind', default: 'the motion of the element', description: 'The motion the element plays in place of its own, at each trigger. none turns it off.' },
      { name: 'motion-[name]', values: 'a defined motion, in brackets, or motion-none', description: 'The Tailwind utility that sets --udx-motion. Put a variant in front: md:motion-[fade-on-view].' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2 id="good-to-know">Good to know</h2>
  <GoodToKnow>
    <Fact label="Reduced motion">
      <p>
        Under reduced motion, a reveal shows its element at once, and so does each card of this page. With <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the
        element or an ancestor, the element shows its end state without animating. See <DocLink href="/motion/accessibility">Accessibility</DocLink>.
      </p>
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="Timing">
      <p>
        Without a timing, a reveal moves on the default spatial spring and fades on the default effects spring. The fade that a card names has only
        opacity keyframes, so it fades on the default effects spring.
      </p>
      <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'spring.default.effects']} />
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="Limits">
      <ul>
        <li>If a card must play a presence, such as <code>fade</code>, name it in <code>data-motion</code> in place of the reveal: <code>--udx-motion</code> cannot change the kind of a motion.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fact>
  </GoodToKnow>
</MotionPage>

<script>
  import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

  // The page defines the motion that the second card names from md up, so that it exists before any card can play.
  defineMotion('fade-on-view', {
    verb: 'reveal',
    keyframes: [{ opacity: 0 }, { opacity: 1 }],
  });
</script>
